1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Welcome to the lab for sessions fifteen and sixteen:

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:07,645
data quality, integration and validation in Python.

3
00:00:07,645 --> 00:00:21,600
We run every lecture topic, accuracy, completeness, consistency, validity, integration, duplicates, encoding, validation rules and scaling, on a real dataset, with the code and the output.

4
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,792
This is part A, from the audit to validity.

5
00:00:25,592 --> 00:00:32,348
The roadmap has twelve steps, from a messy merged file to a validated, scaled dataset.

6
00:00:32,348 --> 00:00:45,152
Audit; accuracy; completeness; consistency; validity; integration; duplicates; removal; encoding; validation; scale; and finally the same code on a second dataset.

7
00:00:45,952 --> 00:00:47,385
We use two real files.

8
00:00:47,385 --> 00:00:52,010
The primary is a two-source merge of the New York City Airbnb listings:

9
00:00:52,010 --> 00:01:04,451
source A is the Analytics export with forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five listings, and source B is a Finance export of one thousand of the same listings, with its own price field.

10
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:11,616
Three hundred of those were exported with lowercase borough and room type, and seven hundred are exact copies.

11
00:01:11,616 --> 00:01:16,566
They were concatenated, the way two teams' files often land in one pipeline.

12
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:23,536
The second dataset is Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention, with twelve thousand three hundred thirty rows.

13
00:01:24,336 --> 00:01:31,314
Load the libraries, pandas, numpy, matplotlib and Standard Scaler, then read the lab file.

14
00:01:31,314 --> 00:01:42,168
The columns include I D, name, host, neighbourhood group, room type, price, minimum nights, reviews, availability, and a price U S D column.

15
00:01:42,968 --> 00:01:43,980
Profile first:

16
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:47,737
five commands cover the whole dataset with no loops.

17
00:01:47,737 --> 00:01:53,879
The shape is forty nine thousand eight hundred ninety five rows by seventeen columns.

18
00:01:53,879 --> 00:01:59,659
The total number of missing cells is twenty thousand five hundred seventy three.

19
00:01:59,659 --> 00:02:03,706
Duplicated sum finds seven hundred exact duplicate rows.

20
00:02:03,706 --> 00:02:08,764
And transposing then calling duplicated finds exact duplicate columns:

21
00:02:08,764 --> 00:02:09,992
one, price U S D.

22
00:02:10,792 --> 00:02:15,874
One table gives the type, gaps and distinct values for every column.

23
00:02:15,874 --> 00:02:22,301
Notice ten borough labels for five boroughs, and six room type labels for three types.

24
00:02:22,301 --> 00:02:25,888
The audit already exposes a consistency problem.

25
00:02:26,688 --> 00:02:28,496
Accuracy needs a reference.

26
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:34,859
Our proxy is the median price of listings with the same borough and room type, the peer median.

27
00:02:34,859 --> 00:02:42,160
Group by borough and room type, transform with the median, and compute each price's ratio to its peer median.

28
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:49,728
Sorting by the ratio shows a private room in Queens at ten thousand dollars, against a peer median of fifty nine.

29
00:02:50,528 --> 00:02:54,567
Now flag suspicious values, but do not delete them yet.

30
00:02:54,567 --> 00:02:58,312
Eleven listings are priced at zero dollars a night.

31
00:02:58,312 --> 00:03:02,865
Sixty six are priced more than twenty times their peer median.

32
00:03:02,865 --> 00:03:06,389
Fourteen have a minimum stay longer than a year.

33
00:03:06,389 --> 00:03:08,372
Why flag instead of delete?

34
00:03:08,372 --> 00:03:13,512
The true price is unknown, and deleting real listings biases the data.

35
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:20,048
Record the suspicion in a column and decide on capping or removal in the outlier session.

36
00:03:20,848 --> 00:03:24,580
Completeness asks how much is missing, column by column.

37
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:33,111
Reviews per month and last review are each missing about twenty point six percent, while host name and name are almost complete.

38
00:03:33,111 --> 00:03:38,776
At row level, only seventy nine point three eight percent of rows have no gap at all.

39
00:03:39,576 --> 00:03:40,796
Check the reason.

40
00:03:40,796 --> 00:03:49,339
A cross-tab shows that every listing with zero reviews has no last review date, and every listing with reviews has one.

41
00:03:49,339 --> 00:03:51,564
That is structural, not random.

42
00:03:51,564 --> 00:03:55,655
A listing with no reviews cannot have a last-review date.

43
00:03:55,655 --> 00:04:03,408
The correct fill, which we cover in the next session, is reviews per month equals zero, not the column mean.

44
00:04:04,208 --> 00:04:05,963
Consistency across rows:

45
00:04:05,963 --> 00:04:07,937
one borough, two spellings.

46
00:04:07,937 --> 00:04:19,783
Value counts shows ten labels for five real boroughs, with lowercase manhattan, brooklyn, queens, bronx and staten island sitting beside the proper-case versions.

47
00:04:19,783 --> 00:04:26,072
A group by price therefore gives ten groups instead of five, splitting the statistics.

48
00:04:26,872 --> 00:04:28,816
Consistency across columns:

49
00:04:28,816 --> 00:04:34,000
the price and price U S D columns are equal, the same fact stored twice.

50
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,584
Across related fields:

51
00:04:35,584 --> 00:04:40,552
zero rows break the rule that zero reviews means no last review date.

52
00:04:40,552 --> 00:04:41,632
Across sources:

53
00:04:41,632 --> 00:04:48,400
the same listing I D appears as Manhattan Private room in one source and lowercase in another.

54
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:49,821
Validity:

55
00:04:49,821 --> 00:04:54,097
write the rules first, then test every row against every rule.

56
00:04:54,097 --> 00:04:55,546
Here is a rule table:

57
00:04:55,546 --> 00:05:09,271
price at least zero, minimum nights at least one, latitude and longitude inside the city's bounding box, availability between zero and three sixty five, and room type and borough in the allowed sets.

58
00:05:09,271 --> 00:05:17,617
Counting failures per rule, five rows fail the latitude box, three hundred fail room type and three hundred fail borough.

59
00:05:17,617 --> 00:05:20,928
In total, three hundred five rows fail any rule.

60
00:05:21,728 --> 00:05:25,047
A value can pass one check and fail the other.

61
00:05:25,047 --> 00:05:31,181
The private room at ten thousand dollars passes every validity rule, but it is wrong.

62
00:05:31,181 --> 00:05:38,685
A latitude of forty point four nine nine eight fails the box, but it is a real listing in Staten Island.

63
00:05:38,685 --> 00:05:43,881
And lowercase brooklyn fails the set check, yet it is the right borough.

64
00:05:43,881 --> 00:05:48,427
Validity asks, is this possible, and is checked against a rule.

65
00:05:48,427 --> 00:05:52,973
Accuracy asks, is this correct, and is checked against reality.

66
00:05:52,973 --> 00:05:54,560
Rules need review too.

